@startuml tlb_memory_access
!include ../../../00_SUPPLEMENTARY/diagrams_common/skin.puml
title TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer)

skinparam activity {
    DiamondBackgroundColor #FFF9C4
}

start

:CPU generates
**Virtual Address**;

:Extract **Page Number**;

if (Search in\n**TLB**?) then (hit)
    :Frame# found directly!;
    :No page-table read in this simplified hit path;
else (miss)
    :Consult **Page Table**
    (one-level table in the numeric example);
    :Read page-table entry (m in the example);
    
    if (PTE permits this access?) then (Yes)
        :Update TLB;
    else (No - Page Fault)
        :Page-fault exception
        Demand-zero / CoW / disk-backed / protection case;
        if (Kernel can resolve the fault?) then (yes)
            :Resolve, update page table and retry access;
            :Refresh translation in the TLB;
        else (no)
            :Report unrecoverable access fault;
            stop
        endif
    endif
endif

:Form **Physical Address**
Frame# + Offset;

:Access **Physical Memory**;

stop

legend right
**One-level, no-fault EAT model**
Hit path assumes cached permissions allow the access.
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
α = TLB hit rate (e.g. 98%)
ε = TLB access (e.g. 1 ns)
m = Memory access (e.g. 100 ns)

**EAT = α(ε+m) + (1-α)(ε+2m)**

Example:
0.98×(1+100) + 0.02×(1+200)
= 98.98 + 4.02 = **103 ns**

Without TLB: 200 ns (one table read + data)
With TLB: 103 ns → **48.5% latency reduction**
Speed ratio: 200/103 ≈ 1.94
L-level uncached walk: EAT = ε + m + (1-α)Lm
Real walk caches/parallelism and faults require a richer model.
endlegend

@enduml
